{"returncode":0,"stdout":" "That's the more accurate explanation, then \u2014 not a hold on the number, but a genuine distinction in Google's system between two different phone number slots that are easy to assume are the same thing: < cite index=\"40-1\">a recovery phone is what gets you back in if you forget your password or get locked out, while a separate 2-Step Verification phone number is specifically what receives sign-in and verification codes</cite> \u2014 and multiple long-running Google Account Community threads exist specifically because people discover, the hard way, that these aren't automatically the same number even when they only ever entered one number, one time, years ago.\n\nWhat that means concretely: your number can sit in **Personal info \u2192 Phone number** \u2014 visible, correct, seven years old, exactly as you remember giving it \u2014 while never having been separately added under **Security \u2192 2-Step Verification \u2192 Phone numbers**, which is the specific list \"Verify it's you\" pulls from for sensitive-action codes. Those are two different settings screens in your account, and Google doesn't cross-populate one from the other automatically. That would fully explain what you're seeing: the number is genuinely there, genuinely yours, genuinely not \"lost\" \u2014 it's just filed under the wrong purpose for what this particular screen checks.\n\n**The actual fix, from that Linux laptop you're already logged into:** go to your Google Account \u2192 Security \u2192 2-Step Verification, and look specifically for a \"Phone numbers\" section under there, separate from wherever your recovery number shows. If it's empty or only lists something else, add your number there directly. Given what we found earlier about the 7-day trust window, a newly-added 2SV number likely won't unlock tonight's specific problem immediately \u2014 but it stops this exact wall from happening again, and confirms definitively whether this was the actual cause.",
    "citations": []
  }
]","stderr":""}

